-- title:
--   Fontaine's criterion descends along a bialgebra quotient
-- statement:
--   Let $\mathcal O$ be a commutative ring, $p$ a prime, $k$ a commutative $\mathcal O$-algebra, and let $\mathcal S'$, $\mathcal S$, $\mathcal R$ be commutative $\mathcal O$-bialgebras. Let $\varphi \colon \mathcal S' \to \mathcal S$ and $q \colon \mathcal S \to \mathcal R$ be morphisms of $\mathcal O$-bialgebras with $q$ surjective, and assume that the kernel of $q$ as an $\mathcal O$-algebra map is the ideal of $\mathcal S$ generated by $\varphi$ of the augmentation ideal $\ker(\varepsilon_{\mathcal S'})$. Let $Y$ be a commutative $\mathcal O$-algebra. For a commutative $\mathcal O$-bialgebra $A$ and a ring map $\pi\colon \mathcal B \to A$, write $\mathrm{wittHom}$ for the additive subgroup of truncated Witt vectors $x$ of length $n$ over $A$ that are primitive, i.e. $W_n(\Delta)(x) = W_n(\iota_1)(x) + W_n(\iota_2)(x)$, and $\mathrm{fontaineKer}$ for the additive subgroup of those $a$ of the form $a = \mathrm{truncate}_n(W(\pi)(X))$ for some Witt vector $X$ over $\mathcal B$ whose $(n-1)$-st ghost component lies in $(p^n)$. Say a $k$-algebra map out of $k \otimes_{\mathcal O} A$ satisfies Fontaine's condition if it carries every primitive truncated Witt vector of every length $n$ lying in $\mathrm{fontaineKer}$ for $\mathrm{includeRight}\colon A \to k \otimes_{\mathcal O} A$ into $\mathrm{fontaineKer}$ for $\mathrm{includeRight}\colon Y \to k \otimes_{\mathcal O} Y$, via the induced map on truncated Witt vectors. Assume: every $k$-algebra map $k \otimes_{\mathcal O} \mathcal S \to k \otimes_{\mathcal O} Y$ satisfying Fontaine's condition is the base change of an $\mathcal O$-algebra map $\mathcal S \to Y$; and two $\mathcal O$-algebra maps $\mathcal S' \to Y$ with equal base change along $\mathcal O \to k$ coincide. Then every $k$-algebra map $\chi \colon k \otimes_{\mathcal O} \mathcal R \to k \otimes_{\mathcal O} Y$ satisfying Fontaine's condition is the base change of an $\mathcal O$-algebra map $\mathcal R \to Y$.
--
--   This is the third step of Fontaine's classification of finite flat commutative $p$-group schemes over $W(k)$ by finite Honda systems, in the form used by Conrad: Fontaine's description of the $Y$-points of a group scheme in terms of the special fibre together with the kernel of $w$ passes from a group scheme and a subgroup to the scheme-theoretic quotient, here expressed bialgebraically through the hypothesis that $\ker q$ is generated by the image of the augmentation ideal of $\mathcal S'$. It is used in the deformation-theoretic part of the development, for the passage from levels of a $p$-divisible group to $p$-torsion group schemes and from there to the case of $\mathbf Z/p$-coefficients.
